hello everyone and welcome to math  simplified i hope all of you are healthy  and happy  today we are going to take a close look  at a special vitamin that is essential  for our skin hair and nails vitamins are  substances that our body needs to grow  and develop normally  most vitamins need to come from food  because the body either does not produce  them or produces very little amounts  different vitamins play different roles  in the body and a person requires  adequate amount of each vitamin to stay  healthy  you may have heard about vitamin a b  vitamin c vitamin d and vitamin k but in  today's video we'll talk about a  forgotten vitamin vitamin h  yes you heard it right vitamin h  you may also know it by its common name  biotin or vitamin b7  well i say biotin is a forgotten vitamin  because most of us don't even know what  it does for us and why it is important  so let's talk about biotin its history  its role in our body the deficiency of  biotin and what you should include in  your diet to increase biotin intake  before we begin we would like to thank  you for your support if you like our  content subscribe to our channel it  helps us grow and make better content  for you  you can also find us on instagram we  post daily facts medical tidbits and  posts that will brighten your day the  links are in the description below  in 1916 scientist w g bateman observed  that a diet high in raw egg whites  caused a toxic disease in dogs cats  rabbits and even humans this was  confirmed in 1927 when researchers  observed that rats who were fed on an  exclusive egg white diet showed symptoms  of brain dysfunction hair loss skin  problems and eventually they died  but it wasn't until 1936 when some  german biochemists isolated the  crystalline substance from boiled egg  yolks they named it biotin because they  believed it was one of the components of  the bios  bios word which means life  biotin is a water soluble vitamin as you  might know our body can store only fat  soluble vitamins such as vitamin a  vitamin d vitamin e and vitamin k  hence biotin needs to be replaced by a  healthy and prudent diet  biotin is also known as vitamin b7 and  also as vitamin h  you might be wondering why vitamin h  well the h stands for har and hot  heart and heart are german words which  means hair and skin  so let's look at few of the important  roles of biotin in our body  number one biotin is an important part  of metabolic pathways  the food we eat goes through a lengthy  process involving various metabolic  pathways which eventually allow the body  to use it as energy  let me simplify this for you take for  example we take a healthy meal  containing adequate carbs good amount of  proteins and fat  once the food reaches our gut various  enzymes start acting on them these  enzymes break the larger macronutrients  into smaller micronutrients  once these nutrients are broken down  into small enough units they are  absorbed into the blood and sent all  over the body for utilization  it is here that biotin plays an  important role such as in  gluconeogenesis  which is a process where new glucose  molecules are synthesized from other  non-carbohydrate sources  while i won't go into the depth of the  gluconeogenesis pathway you just need to  know that biotin containing enzymes are  essential for gluconeogenesis  biotin is also important for amino acid  breakdown amino acids are the smallest  units that make up a protein  amino acids such as leucine require  biotin containing enzymes to be properly  metabolized in the body and to be used  biotin is also important for the  synthesis of fatty acids pads are the  primary way by which our body stores  extra energy to be used later biotin  dependent enzymes are vital for this  process of synthesizing fatty acids  the second important role of biotin in  our body is that it helps in maintaining  the health of our skin  hair and nails  keratin is a basic protein that makes up  your hair skin and nails  biotin improves the keratin biostructure  and eventually helps in maintaining the  hair and nail health  to study whether biotin is useful in  hair growth a 2015 study was done  over three months adult women who  reported thinning of hair were taken as  subjects 30 women were given oral biotin  rich marine protein supplements and 30  women were given placebos the scalp was  photographed digitally before starting  the supplements and the images were  compared to the scalp images taken 90  days later of the same areas  the results reported that biotin treated  subjects achieved a significant increase  in the number of hairs within the target  area biotin use also resulted in  significantly less hair shedding there  were no reported adverse events and the  researchers concluded that biotin  promotes hair growth and decreases hair  loss in women suffering from thinning  and loss of hair  number three  biotin is important in pregnancy  according to research a mild biotin  deficiency is common in pregnancy even  though the diets of most people are  biotin adequate it is reported that over  50 percent of all pregnant females in  u.s are marginally biotin deficient  this occurs because the vitamin is  broken down faster as a result a  pregnant female may require more biotin  than normal  number four biotin reduces blood sugar  levels  biotin is important in the metabolic  process of energy its deficiency can  negatively affect the glucose balance of  the body  in case of type 2 diabetes the body has  excessive blood sugar levels mostly due  to dysfunction in action of insulin  hormone  researchers say that a biotin deficiency  disrupts glucose regulation which can  have catastrophic effects for people who  are already struggling to control blood  sugar levels  researchers indicate that a combination  of biotin and chromium improves blood  sugar control in some people with type 2  diabetes the problem is that biotin  alone doesn't seem to have the same  effect and more research is needed to  determine whether supplementing biotin  can actually treat diabetes  now that we know some roles of biotin in  our body let's talk about biotin  deficiency and let's talk about what are  the symptoms of biotin deficiency  it is rare to become biotin deficient as  it is adequately present in most diets  but there are some special cases worth  mentioning this vitamin is especially  needed for gym and fitness enthusiasts  well people who eat raw egg whites are  especially at risk of developing biotin  deficiency this is due to a binding  factor called avidin which binds to  biotin and prevents the gut from  absorbing it  also people who have gut disease or who  are on parental intravenous feeding are  also at risk  some people who are on anti-seizure  medications and long-term antibiotics  are also at the risk of becoming biotin  deficient some of the symptoms of biotin  deficiency include hair loss dry scaly  skin tracks at the corners of the mouth  swollen or magenta colored tongue dry  eyes loss of appetite fatigue and  insomnia  depression  thinning and splitting of hair  brittle nails  glucose dysfunction  and nerve disease leading to numbness  and tingling sensations in hands feet  and legs  now that we know about the symptoms of  biotin deficiency let's talk about how  much biotin do you need daily  one way we measure a particular vitamins  or minerals requirement is by its  recommended daily intake  rda or recommended daily allowance is a  standard dose which is based on a  person's age sex and health status  according to the national institute of  health the daily requirement of biotin  is as follows  in infants biotin requirement is about 5  to 6 micrograms  children who are less than 12 years of  age require 10 to 20 micrograms of  biotin in their diet  teens required 25 micrograms of biotin  in their diet while adults require 30  micrograms in their diet  pregnant and breast feeding females  require a little more like about 30 to  35 micrograms of biotin in their diet  now let me tell you about a few foods to  boost your biotin levels  meats especially kidney and liver meats  are abundant sources of biotin  egg yolk also contains great amounts of  biotin  other sources include cheese soybeans  and peanuts  green leafy vegetables also contain good  amounts of biotin  other sources include cauliflower  mushrooms yeasts nuts and bananas  the bacteria in your gut also produce  minute quantities of biotin which is  then absorbed in your blood  heat can reduce biotin's efficacy so opt  for raw or minimally processed tissues  high dose biotin is being used as a  treatment for multiple sclerosis  multiple sclerosis or ms is an  autoimmune disease of the brain spinal  cord and nerve  this is because it damages the  protective functional covering of nerves  themselves  this covering is known as myelin  biotin is supposed to play an important  role in the production of myelin it  controls the key enzyme responsible for  myelin synthesis  so researchers are currently studying  the role of around 300 milligram per day  biotin  in treatment of multiple sclerosis that  is around 10 000 times the recommended  daily intake  the clinical trial results are mixed a  2019 review concluded that a further  investigation of the association between  multiple sclerosis symptoms and biotin  should be undertaken biotin holds a  great future prospect of treating this  serious disease  if you feel that your diet is lacking in  biotin or you just want to supplement  your biotin levels you can choose over  the counter biotin supplements also  these are very famous for their hair  benefits  i will link a few good biotin  supplements in the description box down  below so you can check them out  so friends this was all about this video  i hope you liked this video and make  sure to subscribe to our channel for our  upcoming videos thank you so much and  i'll see you in the next one
 

